Spec: Feature Case-Insensitive Usernames

Purpose:
  Treat login IDs that differ only in letter case (e.g. `q225265` and `Q225265`) as the same user across IAM user management, the WebAuthn ceremonies and OIDC sign-in, by storing and comparing usernames in one canonical form.

Platform: Rust/Axum/PostgreSQL

References:
  ../../../mitm-2.sdd
  ../../../core-layer/core-layer.sdd
  ../../../core-layer/specs/security.sdd
  ../../../core-layer/mitm_http-server/specs/api_spec.sdd
  ../oidc-authentication/oidc-authentication.sdd
  ../webauthn-only-auth/webauthn-only-auth.sdd

Owns:
  ./**
  /migrations/005_username_uppercase.sql

Can modify:
  /migrations/setup.sql
  /core-layer/mitm_common/**
  /core-layer/mitm_http-server/**
  /core-layer/mitm_iam-server/**

Must:
  The canonical username is the input with surrounding whitespace removed and ASCII letters converted to uppercase.
  A canonical username matches `^[A-Z0-9._-]{1,64}$`.
  Every username entering the core-layer from a client, the IdP or the configuration is canonicalized by @mitm_common::username::normalize before it is stored or compared.
  `admin_users.username` and `user_sessions.os_user` hold canonical usernames only; the database rejects other values for `admin_users.username`.
  The OIDC `sub` claim is canonicalized after the ID token has been validated.
  `POST /api/v1/iam/users` answers `400` for a username that does not match the format and `409` for an existing username.
  The migration aborts without changes when existing usernames collide after canonicalization.

Tasks:
  [x] #1 Add @mitm_common::username::normalize with unit tests.
  [x] #2 Canonicalize usernames in core-layer/mitm_http-server (IAM create, WebAuthn register and login start, OIDC callback) and answer duplicates with `409`.
  [x] #3 Canonicalize configured admin usernames in core-layer/mitm_iam-server.
  [ ] #4 Add migration `005_username_uppercase.sql` and append it to `setup.sql`.
  [x] #5 Update oidc-authentication.sdd, API documentation and CHANGELOGs.

Done when:
  `cargo build` and `cargo test` for core-layer succeed and logins with `q225265` and `Q225265` resolve to the same `admin_users` row.

Scenario: login with different case
  Given `admin_users` contains `Q225265`
  When a client starts a WebAuthn login with `q225265`
  Then the ceremony is started for `Q225265`

Scenario: OIDC subject in lowercase
  Given `admin_users` contains the active user `Q225265`
  And the validated ID token has `sub` `q225265`
  When the OIDC callback completes
  Then a session for `Q225265` is issued

Scenario: case-variant duplicate
  Given `admin_users` contains `Q225265`
  When an administrator creates the user `q225265`
  Then the request is answered with `409`

Scenario: invalid username
  When an administrator creates the user `max mustermann`
  Then the request is answered with `400`
  And no user is stored
